  "name": "LACTATE DEHYDROGENASE - LDH FLUID",
  "code": "1137",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) activity is present in all cells of the body with the highest concentrations in the heart, </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">liver, muscle, kidney, lung, and erythrocytes.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) to serum LDH ratio above 0.6 are most consistent with exudative effusions. </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Peritoneal fluid LDH above 220 U/L suggests secondary, rather than spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, in conjunction </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">with other laboratory, imaging, and clinical findings. Synovial fluid LDH may be elevated greater than plasma or serum </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">LDH due to inflammatory causes. Values should be interpreted in conjunction with other clinical findings.(7)</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">All other fluids: LDH may be used to differentiate transudative from exudative effusions. The decision limits are not well </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">defined in fluids other than pleural fluid and should be interpreted in conjunction with other clinical findings.</span></p>",
